[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Designing for Change: Loose Coupling and High Cohesion"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In modern software architecture, change is the only constant. Systems that survive over time are those built to embrace change rather than resist it. Two foundational principles guide this architectural resilience: Loose Coupling and High Cohesion."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding High Cohesion"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Cohesion refers to the degree to which the responsibilities of a single software module form a meaningful, focused unit. High cohesion means that classes or modules are designed with a single, well-defined purpose."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Easier to understand: Developers can read a class and immediately grasp its domain responsibility."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Localized changes: When a business requirement changes, developers only need to modify a single, predictable module."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reusability: Focused modules are inherently easier to extract and reuse across different parts of an application."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Loose Coupling"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Coupling measures the degree of interdependence between software modules. Loose coupling ensures that modules interact through abstract interfaces rather than concrete implementations, minimizing the ripple effect of changes."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reduced dependencies: Changes to the internal logic of one module do not break consuming modules."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Improved testability: Loosely coupled components can be easily mocked and tested in isolation."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Parallel development: Different teams can work on different modules simultaneously without blocking each other."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Practical Code Example"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Consider an order processing system. In a tightly coupled design, the OrderProcessor directly instantiates a concrete MySQL database and a specific email sender. Here is how we refactor this using Dependency Injection for loose coupling and high cohesion:"},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"interface DatabaseRepository {
  fun save(data: String)
}

interface NotificationService {
  fun send(message: String)
}

class OrderProcessor(
  private val db: DatabaseRepository,
  private val notifier: NotificationService
) {
  fun processOrder(orderId: String) {
    db.save(orderId)
    notifier.send("Order processed: $orderId")
  }
}"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"By depending on interfaces rather than concrete implementations, the OrderProcessor remains completely agnostic of how the database saves data or how the notification is delivered. This makes the system profoundly adaptable to future changes."}]
